<p>
  this site was created initially as a simple link-in-bio site for my projects and commission information, like a
  fancier <a rel="external" href="https://carrd.co/">carrd</a> or
  <a rel="external" href="https://linktr.ee/">Linktree</a>, but over time it’s shifted to being a very personal creative
  outlet for me. now it’s as much of a creative project in and of itself. i always loved the concept of the internet
  being used as a means to express yourself and connect with others. these days that usually means social media, but i
  was never great with social media for whatever reason and still find it very, very exhausting. making this site led to
  the discovery of the independent web and all the other adjacent movements (the Indie Web, Web Revival, Small Web,
  etc.) which has felt a lot less suffocating and brings (back) a lot more personality to the Web, i think.
</p>

<p>
  this site is coded by my real human/rabbit hands with <a rel="external" href="https://neovim.io/">Neovim</a> and built
  using <a rel="external" href="https://www.11ty.dev/">Eleventy</a>, you can find the source code at its
  <a rel="external" href="https://github.com/dstbnnybdrm/dstbnnysite">GitHub repo</a>. this site is proudly hosted on
  <a rel="external" href="https://neocities.org/">Neocities</a> and deployed using Bret Comnes’
  <a rel="external" href="https://github.com/bcomnes/deploy-to-neocities">Deploy to Neocities</a> Github Action. to
  <a rel="external" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dither">dither</a> images i use makeworld’s
  <a rel="external" href="https://github.com/makew0rld/didder">didder</a> command-line tool. there is
  <strong>zero</strong> generative “AI” use on this site and it makes me a little bit sad to have to disclose that these
  days lol <span role="img" aria-label="smile face with tear">:')</span>.
  <a rel="external" href="https://badgraph1csghost.neocities.org/trillion">GenAI sucks</a>.
</p>

<p>
  the design of this site is kind of an exercise in doing lot with a little? as such i sort of roughly follow the
  principles of <a rel="external" href="https://brutalist-web.design/">brutalist web design</a> (as outlined by David
  Copeland). i use text and <span class="letter-spacing-caps">ASCII</span> art a lot as visual embellishments, i just
  like the aesthetic. i always thought, like, the simplicity of
  <abbr title="Command-Line Interface">CLI</abbr> applications looked really cool. i also take lots of inspiration from
  personal sites from the 90s up to the early 2000s and use some of the common tropes found among them for fun and
  nostalgia but also as a nod to a less serious and more playful era of the internet. the main hub page (viewed on
  desktop) is modelled after GeoCities sites that used <code>&lt;framesets&gt;</code> for their page layouts, though
  instead it uses an <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> for the main content à la some personal sites i’ve seen from the 2000s
  (and now!).
</p>

<p>
  the colours used in this site are colour-picked and slightly tweaked from various frames in
  <span class="letter-spacing-caps">1992</span>’s <cite>Sailor Moon</cite>—but the version tinted pink from film
  degradation, not the original! though it wasn’t the original artistic intent, i always thought the pink hue was really
  endearing and warm feeling. for the light theme i picked mainly colours from scenes in Usagi’s bedroom (because, well
  y’know) and for the dark theme i used colours from night shots, typically from outside.
</p>

<p>
  i try to keep the site
  <a rel="external" href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG2AA-Conformance"
    ><abbr title="Web Content Accessibility Guidelines Level AA">WCAG-AA</abbr></a
  >
  compliant, i can’t verify whether it’s fully compliant or not, so i won’t claim it to be, but i do strive to keep
  accessibility in mind at all times. i periodically test this site using a screen reader,
  <span class="letter-spacing-caps"><a rel="external" href="https://www.nvaccess.org/">NVDA</a></span> for Windows. this
  site should be navigable using just a keyboard, and also <em>should</em> remain largely functional without JavaScript.
  if you have any issues with regards to accessibility please feel free to send me
  <a href="/about.html#elsewhere">a message</a>
  about it!
</p>

